1.m:
From the center, one should trace a circle with the measure of a side;

With an excess of one-third, [that is] the entire circle together [with the excess].

In the square, one should drop the cord diagonally from the center;

Having traced with half of that, that indeed is the semicircle. \8\

2.m:
At the loop, at half a cord are the buttocks; at two is the western axial line, at two;

The eastern half is then at one and a half; then at one and a half is an intermediate mark;

At half is a shoulder; another is at one and a half; then at one and a half is the northern shoulder;

Then at a cubit is the loop: this is the Māruta and Vāruṇa altar. \5\

3.m:
If a crested altar is to be made, one should raise two and a half from the edge of the altar;

Eight finger-breadths shall be half of that, for the perfection of the divine altar;

One should direct that eastward, and one should know them as the crested altar. \8\

4.m:
The measure of a finger-breadth is six barleycorns joined side by side;

A span is ten finger-breadths; a span is twelve finger-breadths;

A cubit shall be two spans; a fathom is of four cords. \4\

9.m:
One hundred and eighty-six is the first [layer]; the second is one hundred and seventy;

the third is one hundred and thirteen, the wise say;

the fourth is one hundred and one, and three bricks are traditionally remembered [in addition];

three hundred and fifty-six is the highest brick layer.

16.m:
One should delineate the navel with half a man-length, then within the range of the brick layer;

The rim in excess of the spokes, with a sixty-third [part] the axle-box;

With thirty together with twenty, and with four and a half in excess;

Or one should measure the middle in finger-breadths; with a twentieth one should make the drawing. \4\
